        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much does a tankless water heater cost installed?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most homes pay $3,000 to $6,500 for a gas tankless replacing a tank, and $1,500 to $3,500 for an electric whole-house unit when the existing panel can supply it. Point-of-use electric units install for $300 to $1,200. Gas line upsizing, venting and electrical upgrades are what push a project toward the top of each range."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why is tankless installation so expensive?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Because a tankless heater rarely drops into a tank's connections. Gas units need far more fuel, often a 3/4-inch line, and new sealed venting, since the old flue cannot be reused. Electric units need heavy dedicated circuits. A condensate drain, permits and old tank removal add more. In new construction, most of those costs shrink or vanish."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a tankless water heater worth the cost?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "On energy savings alone, rarely; a gas unit saves about $80 to $115 a year against a standard tank. Counting its roughly 20-year life against two tanks over the same period, the lifecycle cost is close to even. It is worth it if you will stay long-term, have suitable gas supply and value endless hot water."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is electric or gas tankless cheaper?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Electric tankless is cheaper to buy and install if your electrical service can handle it, since there is no venting or gas line. Gas tankless is usually cheaper to run where gas prices are moderate, and it handles cold climates far better. If you would need a service upgrade for electric, gas or a heat pump tank usually wins."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is there still a tax credit for tankless water heaters?",
